Fillets: a concave rounding whose chain runs through a wall's concave
corner tighter than the ball (mitcad#1371).

The case: a rod (the chain's common face U, a cylinder) and a wall
standing on it whose plan runs through arcs of 0.4 (convex), 1.25
(concave), 12 (convex), 1.25 and 0.4, tangent in turn, rounded with 3
along their foot. No ball of radius R touches a concave arc K of radius
rk < R: its centre would run round K's axis at a radius of rk - R < 0.
OCCT's walk went into K and failed short of its end ("the walk between
the two faces fails"), on the replayed and on the stored body.

The construction is the one the bodies stored in the files have: K is
itself the rolling ball of radius rk between the walls Fa and Fb it is
tangent to. The roundings A (along Fa) and B (along Fb) run on past the
ball that touches U, Fa and Fb at once, each until its contact on its
wall reaches K's edge; the ball of radius rk rolls on down from K,
between the wall and the rounding whose end on its wall is the higher
(the strip), then between the two roundings (the triangle), until their
contacts on U meet at P, where the ball of radius R touches all three
faces, the two roundings touch and the ball's section vanishes. On the
reported design (R 3, rk 1.25) every section of the stored strip and
triangle is a circle of radius 1.25 whose centre lies 1.75 = R - rk from
both roundings' ball centres (within 5e-5 mm of the stored faces'
fitting), the triangle's curves on the roundings are that ball's contacts
(which is why they are neither sections nor planar), and the circle
between the strip and the triangle is that ball's section where its
contact on the wall crosses A's contact.

- ChFi3d_Builder::SplitAtTightConcaveArcs (new, at the start of Compute,
  after SplitAtTightCorners): a concave contour of constant radius R
  whose chain has an edge Ek between U and a cylinder K of radius below
  R, K's axis on the ball's side, K tangent along its edges Ka and Kb
  (lines along its axis, from Ek's vertices) to the faces Fa and Fb of
  the chain's edges before and after Ek, is split there: the runs of
  edges between such arcs become contours of their own, their ends at
  the arcs of status ChFiDS_Tangent, the arcs left out.
- ChFi3d_Builder::ExtendAtTightConcaveArcs (new, in Compute after
  ExtentAnalyse): the ends of contours at such an arc (two contours of
  one radius ending at its two vertices) walk on past their vertex by two
  radii at least (ExtentOneCorner's half spine was too short for the arc
  of 0.4: its walk stopped 0.11 short of K's edge), so that each contact
  on its wall reaches K's edge, where the walk stops. Other ends are left
  as they are.
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformTightConcaveCorners (new, after
  PerformTightCorners, before the corners at vertices), for each such
  arc and the two stripes ending at its vertices (other concave ends of
  status ChFiDS_Tangent are passed over):
  - where each stripe's contact on its wall crosses K's edge (W, at a
    height along K's axis); L the stripe whose W is the lower, H the
    other;
  - P: on L's contact with U, where the ball's centre is R from H's wall
    (the ball touching U, Fa and Fb); P lies on H's contact with U too;
  - the sections of the ball of radius rk, at heights along K's axis,
    solved by Newton's method on the supports' surfaces (the centre rk
    along each support's normal on the ball's side, the two centres one,
    at the height): from K's section at H's W, on L's wall and H's blend,
    down to where the contact on L's wall crosses L's contact there (Q,
    Q2 on H's blend); then on both blends down to the apex, the ball at
    P (its centre rk along U's normal at P);
  - the strip and the triangle: B-spline surfaces through the sections'
    arcs (nine points each), the triangle's last section the point P;
  - the curves (their curves on the faces and surfaces projected and
    checked to lie on them, else made point by point, each in its face's
    or its first point's period: a contact starting on a torus's seam
    came out a period off, and so did a section starting on a cylinder's):
    K's section (on K and the strip), the strip's contacts
    (on L's wall and H's blend), the section Q-Q2 (on the strip and the
    triangle), the triangle's contacts (on both blends), a degenerated
    curve at P; L's stripe ends on the triangle's contact from Q to P,
    H's on one curve from W to P along both contacts on its blend (its
    face's edges are the strip's and the triangle's pieces); the
    stripes' ends are in the data structure as the corners place them;
  - K is cut along its section, the part towards U taken away; L's wall
    loses the sliver between K's edge, the strip's contact and L's
    contact; the strip and the triangle are corner stripes (the strip on
    L's wall and H's blend, the triangle on both blends), generated from
    Ek.
  Where both W lie at one height there is no strip and the triangle
  starts at K's section.
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformTightCorners (0970) leaves concave contours'
  ends to PerformTightConcaveCorners.

On the design's stored body before the item: valid, the result on the
stored body after it and back within 0.0012 mm about the chain, the
change of volume 15.038258 against the file's 15.038261 mm3; before, the
fillet failed. Synthetic (the rod and the wall of the case, OCCT alone):
valid at radii 1.5, 2, 2.4, 3 and 4, no material of the body lost; at 3
the corner's points within 6e-5 mm of the stored body's, the triangle
3.74149 and the strip 0.68399 mm² (the file's 3.74165 and 0.68392). Before: at 3 and 4 the fillet failed; at
1.5 and 2 the walks went on over the concave arc (0160's bridge) and
added 2 % more and 0.4 % less material; at 2.4 OCCT gave a result the
checker passes that is not the body rounded (its cut from the body is
nearly the whole body). A
pocket's floor rounded with 0.8 past corner arcs of 0.4 and 0.5 (the
inverse of the case, mitcad#1368's synthetic pocket): valid, the corners
the arcs' own balls, 0.044 mm3 less material than OCCT's walk round them
added.

